now syrians head to the polls today to  vote in presidential elections  only voters in government-held areas of  the war-torn country can cast their  ballot  the opposition claims the poll is a  farce it says president bashar al-assad  who is running for a fourth  seven year term is preordained to win  it is the second presidential election  since the civil war erupted  ten years ago bashar al-assad's face is  all over the syrian capital damascus his  campaign posters dwarf those of the  other two candidates cleared by the  legislature to compete  the final vote tally is expected to show  similar dominance by assad  so why is everyone so sure of an assad  victory to start with  not all syrians can participate only  residents of areas under government  control can vote  in the country that has the largest  number of displaced people in the world  those in rebel-held districts are  ineligible and the autonomous kurdish  regions in the north are not  participating  millions of syrians have fled the  country during its years of civil war  some of those living abroad can vote  like here in lebanon  on the other hand germany has forbidden  the syrian consulate  from registering voters president bashar  al-assad is running against two other  men  abdullah salum abdullah and mahmoud  ahmad  marie who stand very little chance of  winning  they qualified for the vote after an  extremely restrictive process  in parliament all candidates  must have the support of 35 members of  parliament  which is overwhelmingly dominated by  assad's baptist party  candidates must have lived in syria  continuously for 10 years  excluding anyone from the diaspora and  they must be married to a syrian citizen  the united nations has said it is not  involved in the election  and the u.s says without un involvement  it won't recognize the results  syrian media says the regime has invited  observers  but from regime-friendly countries like  russia iran china and venezuela  the last elections were held in 2014 at  the height of the civil war  and saw assad win in a landslide in a  vote that was also widely condemned by  the u.s and the eu  as neither free nor fair  let's get some analysis on this now with  a journalist kristen helbach one of  germany's leading experts  on the middle east and syria kristen  thank you for joining us we saw  there's pretty much no doubt that  syria's president bashar al-assad  will be the winner here he will get  another seven year term in office  is there anything that we should watch  out for that could come as a surprise in  this election  i don't think there's going to be any  surprise president bashar al-assad needs  this election to legitimize his rule  internally and externally because  especially his very strong ally russia  is seeking the rehabilitation of the  assad regime internationally  because syria lies in ruins it needs  money for reconstruction  so what vladimir putin aims at is  reconstruction being paid by the west by  the europeans and the americans so they  want to  put some dynamic into this  rehabilitation of the regime  by claiming this election to be some  kind of democratic by putting two other  candidates there basically  which doesn't make it any more  democratic than it used to be without  candidates  okay but let's talk about that  legitimacy domestically at home  because as you said the country is in  ruins the economy is in free fall  how much support is there for assad  beyond the propaganda that we see  it is very important to understand that  this election is an opportunity for  syrians to prove their loyalty to the  regime  and this is important in a dictatorship  and in a police state  because it gives you security it could  save your life it could save you from  detention  it could bring you some more  humanitarian aid it could bring you a  permission by the secret service that  you need to  maybe get back to your own house so many  syrians use this actually  to say that they are you know on the  side of the president obviously  because they are afraid of being  detained or  being persecuted so this is very  important what we have seen during the  last month is  is a growing discontent and even critics  by  his own supporters by people in the  media journalists judges  lawyers civil servants who have spoken  out especially on social media  on the very bad economic conditions and  the mismanagement of the government  itself  so these people were detained and they  were just released in  just before the elections in this  presidential amnesty  so this is the message to the people  living there look you  don't you are not supposed to criticize  what i'm doing in this country  just go and elect me for now but it's  important to see you know to understand  these pictures when you see all these  people rallying  running for us this is a way to really  save your life or to make  you uh you know to gain a little bit  from this regime and its methods in the  country  and this 10 years after the civil war  erupted in syria  do you think that this election is also  bashar al-assad trying to  take this as a turning point to say the  war has been won  now we look into the future  well he's definitely sending a different  signals  different messages with this first of  all he's saying that he doesn't care  about any un-led process  because there is a constitutional  committee that is supposed to meet in  geneva and that it's supposed to um  create a new constitution first and then  have democratic elections under you and  surveillance bashar assad  delayed the work of this committee for  several months so the last meeting was  in january  and it ended without any result so he's  saying i don't care for you and  processes i'm just going to stay here  he's also saying to russia that he's not  going to go anywhere in a deal you know  that maybe vladimir putin would broker  with the west saying that okay we'll  let bashar assad go and look for  somebody else who could replace him  he's basically saying that he's going to  stay where he is he is dependent  on the people who keep him in power  which is important to understand  it's iran and russia internationally  it's militia leaders  warlords and the business elite  internally all these people want to be  rewarded by assad himself  which makes syria basically a mafia  state because these people are enriching  themselves by bribes by  controlling the black market by  smuggling things by taking humanitarian  aid for their own militia members  so and this is basically what these  regimes supporters are seeing themselves  and what they were criticizing  but bashar al-assad cannot stabilize the  country because he needs these people  who kept him in power until now because  he needs them in the future  to keep him there journalist kristen  helberg thank you very much for that  analysis  my pleasure  you
 

